SDRangel segfaults when a configuration is loaded twice in quick succession,
which is easy to do by accident on first run: with no saved settings SDRangel
populates the bundled example configurations and opens the Configurations dialog
straight away, and that dialog calls on_configurationLoad_clicked() from both
on_configurationsTree_itemActivated() (double-click or Enter) and accept() (the
OK button). A double-click followed by OK, or simply a second click on Load
while the first is still running, therefore starts two LoadConfigurationFSMs.

MainWindow::loadConfiguration() had no guard, so the two run concurrently. The
second FSM's RemoveAllWorkspacesFSM deletes the workspaces, device sets and GUI
widgets that the first FSM is still walking, and the first then dereferences
freed objects. The victim depends on timing and on which configuration is being
loaded - the QProgressDialog in LoadConfigurationFSM::loadDeviceSetSettings(),
or the Map feature's QQuickWidget, have both been observed. Reproduces 3/3 with
two loads and 0/2 with one.

This serialises the loads: a request arriving while one is in flight is refused
with a warning. The in-flight FSM is tracked through a QPointer, which clears
itself if the FSM is destroyed without finishing, so a load that goes wrong
cannot wedge configuration loading permanently.

LoadConfigurationFSM::m_waitBox becomes a QPointer for the same reason: the
progress dialog is created with WA_DeleteOnClose and the FSM outlives it, so the
existing "if (m_waitBox)" checks in the state handlers were testing a pointer
that could already dangle.

--- a/sdrgui/mainwindow.h	2026-08-19 12:28:39.000000000 -0600
+++ b/sdrgui/mainwindow.h	2026-09-21 11:54:20.886653667 -0600
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
 #include <QFinalState>
 #include <QSignalTransition>
 #include <QProgressDialog>
+#include <QPointer>
 
 #include "settings/mainsettings.h"
 #include "util/message.h"
@@ -231,7 +232,10 @@
 private:
 
     const Configuration *m_configuration;
-    QProgressDialog *m_waitBox;
+    // The dialog is created with WA_DeleteOnClose and this FSM outlives it,
+    // so a bare pointer can dangle between states. QPointer makes the null
+    // checks in the state handlers mean what they are written to mean.
+    QPointer<QProgressDialog> m_waitBox;
 
     RemoveAllWorkspacesFSM *m_removeAllWorkspacesFSM;
 
@@ -346,6 +350,7 @@
 #endif
 
     bool m_settingsSaved;               // Records if settings have already been saved in response to a QCloseEvent
+    QPointer<LoadConfigurationFSM> m_configurationLoadFSM; // In-flight load started by loadConfiguration(), if any
     const MainParser& m_parser;
 
 	void loadSettings();
--- a/sdrgui/mainwindow.cpp	2026-08-19 12:28:39.000000000 -0600
+++ b/sdrgui/mainwindow.cpp	2026-09-21 11:54:20.887092693 -0600
@@ -1924,6 +1924,21 @@
         (int)configuration->getFeatureSetPreset().getFeatureCount()
     );
 
+    // ConfigurationsDialog calls on_configurationLoad_clicked() from both
+    // on_configurationsTree_itemActivated() (double-click or Enter) and accept()
+    // (the OK button), so a double-click followed by OK - or simply a second click
+    // on Load while the first is still running - starts a second LoadConfigurationFSM.
+    // The two then overlap: the second one's RemoveAllWorkspacesFSM deletes the
+    // workspaces, device sets and GUI widgets the first is still walking, and the
+    // first goes on to dereference freed objects. Serialize them instead.
+    // The QPointer clears itself if the FSM dies without finishing, so a load that
+    // goes wrong cannot wedge this permanently.
+    if (!m_configurationLoadFSM.isNull())
+    {
+        qWarning("MainWindow::loadConfiguration: a configuration is already being loaded - ignoring this request");
+        return;
+    }
+
     QProgressDialog *waitBox = nullptr;
 
     if (fromDialog)
@@ -1938,6 +1953,8 @@
     }
 
     LoadConfigurationFSM *fsm = new LoadConfigurationFSM(this, configuration, waitBox);
+    m_configurationLoadFSM = fsm;
+    connect(fsm, &LoadConfigurationFSM::finished, this, [this]() { m_configurationLoadFSM.clear(); });
     connect(fsm, &LoadConfigurationFSM::finished, fsm, &LoadConfigurationFSM::deleteLater);
     fsm->start();
     }
